marble-device-support (1.0.0-4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Ship the Bluetooth address restore: marble-bt-mac.service derives the
    Bluetooth address from the factory WLAN MAC in persist (wlan0 minus one on
    the last octet, the Xiaomi convention seen on boards that also carry
    persist/wlan_bt/bt.mac) and pushes it through 'btmgmt public-addr'. The
    stock NV blob qca/wcnhpnv21g.bin has no BD_ADDR, so without this the
    controller reports 00:00:00:00:00:00, the kernel keeps hci0
    HCI_UNCONFIGURED and 'bluetoothctl list' is empty even though dmesg shows
    the QCA firmware download completing. The address is volatile, so this
    runs on every boot, after bluetooth.service.

 -- GhfunNiHe <131235947+GhfunNiHe@users.noreply.github.com>  Thu, 15 Oct 2026 04:35:00 +0800

marble-device-support (1.0.0-3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Ship the factory WLAN MAC restore: marble-wlan-mac.service mounts the
    Android persist partition read-only, copies qca6490/wlan_mac.bin into
    /lib/firmware/ath11k/WCN6855/hw2.1/ and then modprobes ath11k_pci
    (blacklisted in usr/lib/modprobe.d/marble-wlan.conf so udev cannot load
    it earlier). Pairs with the ath11k-load-wlan-mac-from-firmware patch in
    linux-redmi-marble: without it the interface gets a fresh random MAC at
    every boot and NM's "restore the permanent address" fails EADDRNOTAVAIL
    ('config-failed' on connection activation).

 -- GhfunNiHe <131235947+GhfunNiHe@users.noreply.github.com>  Thu, 15 Oct 2026 02:55:00 +0800

marble-device-support (1.0.0-2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Retarget the sensor-registry drop-in at Debian's hexagonrpcd: upstream
    ships it for hexagonrpcd-sdsp.service (the Alpine/pmOS per-DSP unit that
    runs /usr/bin/hexagonrpcd -f /dev/fastrpc-sdsp -d sdsp -s -R /usr/share/qcom/),
    which does not exist in Debian. Debian's hexagonrpcd (source package
    hexagonrpc) has one unit, and /usr/bin/hexagonrpcd is a wrapper script that
    assembles the same command line from hexagonrpcd_device/_dsp/_fw_dir, so the
    drop-in now sets those variables under hexagonrpcd.service.d/.
    As shipped, the drop-in applied to nothing: the daemon would have started on
    the ADSP looking for a droid-juicer registry, and the iio-sensor-proxy
    drop-in ordered it against a unit that never existed.
  * iio-sensor-proxy.service.d/60-marble-sensors.conf now orders against
    hexagonrpcd.service.

